e (a file-backed loop device, or an ext4 volume where files are sparse), nothing breaks. The interactive thing worth knowing: RocksDB by default *preallocates* file space (`use_fallocate` / rate-limited allocator settings), which turns would-be sparse files into fully-allocated ones — so a \"sparse ext4\" partition only stays sparse for RocksDB's files if you disable/limit the preallocation options. Default behavior = allocate-on-write, so the space-savings of sparseness mostly evaporate unless you  …\n70: L62 [A] 2026-10-25T05:20:46 That's the disk inventory result confirming what we already used to close the repartition thread — no new information changes anything: LVM-on-sdb carve-out, Btrfs-dominant with an ext4 seed LV on sda. Nothing to act on; the partition thread stays closed. Still open and awaiting your go: the two concrete next steps from this session's design — (1) WISHLIST row(s) recording the chat-model decision (private Cozo writes, no per-turn mutation; hook-verified tool receipts; batch vectorize+clear as the one mutation; supersedes OD-91, folds into OD-92), and (2) then the prototype slice. Which one do you want first?\n71: L63 [U] 2026-10-25T05:20:52 i would think for this usefase woudl ge allocate on write\n72: L64 [A] 2026-10-25T05:20:55 Yes — allocate-on-write is the right call for this specific workload. Reasoning concretely: - **Your chat turns are small, frequent, and never deleted in place** — per-conversation rows are append-only until the batch capture clears them. Allocate-on-write (the default; `use_fallocate=false`, no rate-limited allocator churn) is the cheapest path for WAL appends and small SST flushes. The LSM compacts over time anyway, so file tails get rewritten either way. - **Sparse \"savings\" would be fake here**: the data is dense (conversations are contiguous, written as they happen), so hole-punching saves nothing you'd actually leave unallocated. Preallocation doesn't waste anything either — the space genuinely gets use